Fix the cosmo evaluation failure now blocking the nightly flake.lock update (PR #678). This is a SECOND, different failure — the earlier services.overseerr issue was fixed in #680 and is done. Normal cosmo PR flow. KEEP COMMENTS EXTREMELY COMPRESSED / high information density — standing maintainer preference.

THE ERROR (from CI run 30642702912, build-nixos (classic-laddie), verbatim):
    error: The option `systemd.services.llama-swap.environment.LLAMA_CACHE' has conflicting definition values:
    - In `...nixpkgs/nixos/modules/services/networking/llama-swap.nix': "/var/cache/llama-swap/huggingface/hub"
    - In `...cosmo/hosts/classic-laddie/default.nix': "/var/cache/llama-swap"
    Use `lib.mkForce value' or `lib.mkDefault value' to change the priority on any of these definitions.

CAUSE (already diagnosed — do not re-investigate): on 2026-07-25 cosmo hand-patched the llama-swap service to give downloaded GGUFs a persistent cache, because the nixpkgs module of the day set neither a CacheDirectory nor LLAMA_CACHE. Upstream has since implemented exactly this, and better. The current nixpkgs module now contains:
    environment = rec {
      XDG_CACHE_HOME = "/var/cache/${config.systemd.services.llama-swap.serviceConfig.CacheDirectory}";
      LLAMA_CACHE = "${XDG_CACHE_HOME}/huggingface/hub";
    };
    serviceConfig = { CacheDirectory = "llama-swap"; DynamicUser = true; };
So cosmo's CacheDirectory line is now redundant (identical value, merges silently) and cosmo's LLAMA_CACHE line conflicts with upstream's more correct HF-hub path.

THE CHANGE — in hosts/classic-laddie/default.nix, DELETE both hand-patched lines (currently ~491-492):
    systemd.services.llama-swap.serviceConfig.CacheDirectory = "llama-swap";
    systemd.services.llama-swap.environment.LLAMA_CACHE = "/var/cache/llama-swap";
and delete the now-obsolete comment block immediately above them (~487-490) that explains why cosmo set them. Do NOT replace them with mkForce/mkDefault — upstream's value is the correct one and should simply win.
Leave the main llama-swap comment block (~line 458) alone if it merely says GGUFs are pulled via -hf into LLAMA_CACHE — that remains true, upstream just sets it now. Only correct it if it specifically claims cosmo sets the cache.
Change NOTHING else about llama-swap, llama-cpp, open-webui or the models.

SAFE TO CHANGE THE PATH: llama-swap has been running since 2026-07-25 and has never served a request — its journal shows no download or model-load events and the root dataset shows no GGUF data. There is no cached model data to migrate, so the path moving from /var/cache/llama-swap to /var/cache/llama-swap/huggingface/hub costs nothing.

VERIFY — this is the important part, because the failure only appears under the BUMPED lock:
1. On the repo's current lock: 'nix flake check' and 'nix build .#nixosConfigurations.classic-laddie.config.system.build.toplevel --dry-run' must pass. (Note: under the CURRENT lock the upstream module does not set these values, so after your deletion nothing sets them — that still evaluates fine, and the gap closes the moment the lock lands. Expect this and do not re-add the lines to 'fix' it.)
2. Against the BUMPED lock, which is what actually reproduces the error: fetch PR #678's flake.lock (e.g. 'gh api repos/patflynn/cosmo/pulls/678/files' or fetch the branch update_flake_lock_action), put that flake.lock in place in a scratch copy, and re-run the classic-laddie dry-run. It MUST pass — that is the proof the conflict is resolved. Report both results. Do not commit the bumped flake.lock; this PR changes only hosts/classic-laddie/default.nix.
Also dry-run makers-nix and johnny-walker for regressions.

PR BODY: state the conflict verbatim, that upstream now implements what cosmo hand-patched on 2026-07-25 (so this is deleting a workaround that upstream has superseded, not losing a feature), that no cached model data exists so the path change is free, and that this unblocks PR #678 which has been stuck since 2026-07-28.
Title: 'fix(classic-laddie): drop llama-swap cache overrides now that nixpkgs sets them'.

